{"from": "text:mishnah-kelim-11-1", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mk-07-03-04-vessel-washings", "grade": "B", "note": "Mishnah Kelim 11:1: 'Metal vessels, whether they are flat or form a receptacle, are susceptible to impurity' - the impurity of the very vessels (cups, pitchers, bronze kettles) Mark's washing list presupposes. Redacted c. 200 CE, so it may not describe 30s CE practice exactly. Regraded C to B per the architect's Mishnah ruling (2026-11-15): the Mishnah names the same practice the claim states."}
{"from": "text:mishnah-terumot-2-3", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mk-07-03-04-vessel-washings", "grade": "B", "note": "Mishnah Terumot 2:3 rules on one who 'immerses [unclean] vessels on Shabbat' - the routine immersion of vessels is assumed. Redacted c. 200 CE, so it may not describe 30s CE practice exactly. Regraded C to B per the architect's Mishnah ruling (2026-11-15): the Mishnah names the same practice the claim states."}
{"from": "text:mishnah-kelim-19-1", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mk-07-03-04-vessel-washings", "grade": "B", "note": "Mishnah Kelim 19:1 discusses dismantling a bed 'in order that he might immerse it' - couches, too, were objects of immersion, the most disputed item of Mark 7:4's list. Redacted c. 200 CE, so it may not describe 30s CE practice exactly. Regraded C to B per the architect's Mishnah ruling (2026-11-15): the Mishnah names the same practice the claim states."}
{"from": "artifact:second-temple-stone-vessels", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mk-07-03-04-vessel-washings", "grade": "C", "note": "Chalk vessels were impervious to impurity while pottery 'could become impure and would need to be discarded or broken' - the purity economy of washable vessels that Mark 7:4 lists (reused from ch 15, where the same artifact serves the hand-washing claim)."}
{"from": "text:tacitus-histories-4-81", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mk-07-33-touch-and-spittle", "grade": "C", "note": "Tacitus, Hist. 4.81: the Alexandrian blind man 'begged Vespasian that he would deign to moisten his cheeks and eye-balls with his spittle' - spittle as a healing agent in the period. A parallel from elsewhere in the empire; it attests the gesture's currency, not the healing."}
{"from": "text:pseudo-clementine-homilies-justa", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mk-07-26-syrophoenician-woman", "grade": "D", "note": "The third-century pseudo-Clementine Homilies give the Syrophoenician woman the name Justa and her daughter Berenice - a reception tradition that knows the Gospel story and cannot be independent evidence for it; named as dependence."}
{"from": "text:talmud-eruvin-53b", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mk-07-34-ephphatha", "grade": "C", "note": "Eruvin 53b jokes that 'Galileans did not pronounce the guttural letters properly' - Galilean Aramaic speech was a recognisable everyday vernacular. Redacted c. 500 CE and it attests the language setting, not that the word 'Ephphatha' was spoken on this occasion."}
{"from": "claim:mk-07-03-04-vessel-washings", "type": "documented_by", "to": "source:wikipedia-mark-7-washings"}
{"from": "claim:mk-07-34-ephphatha", "type": "documented_by", "to": "source:wikipedia-mark-7-ephphatha"}
{"from": "text:pliny-nh-5-tyre-sidon", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mk-07-26-syrophoenician-woman", "grade": "C", "note": "Pliny, NH 5.76, describes Tyre ('the Tyre so famous in ancient times') and glass-making Sidon as standing first-century Phoenician cities - the region the 'Syrophoenician' woman came from (reused from ch 15, where it serves the Tyre-and-Sidon journey). It attests the setting, not the woman."}
